Network Performance

Network performance describes how efficiently and reliably data moves through a network.

For AWS Cloud & DevOps, these six concepts are important because they help explain slow applications, poor connectivity, packet drops, and network-related performance issues.

1. Bandwidth ⭐

Bandwidth is the maximum data-carrying capacity of a network connection over a given period.

It is usually measured in:

Mbps — Megabits per second
Gbps — Gigabits per second
Example

A network connection with 1 Gbps bandwidth has a higher capacity than one with 100 Mbps.

Important

Bandwidth does not mean the actual speed/data being transferred at a particular moment. Actual data transfer can be lower because of latency, congestion, packet loss, protocol overhead, etc.

Interview:

Bandwidth is the maximum capacity of a network connection to carry data.

2. Latency ⭐

Latency is the time taken for data to travel from one point to another.

It is usually measured in milliseconds (ms).

Example
Client ───────────────→ Server
        Network delay

If latency is high, applications may feel slow even when bandwidth is high.

Common causes
Long physical distance
Network congestion
Routing path
Processing delays
Network device delays
Real-world impact

High latency can affect:

Remote connections
Online gaming
Video calls
APIs
Cloud applications
Database communication

Interview:

Latency is the delay experienced while data travels through a network.

3. Throughput ⭐

Throughput is the actual amount of data successfully transferred through a network over a period of time.

It is usually measured in Mbps or Gbps.

Bandwidth vs Throughput
Bandwidth  = Maximum possible capacity
Throughput = Actual achieved data transfer

For example, a connection may have high bandwidth but achieve lower throughput because of congestion, packet loss, protocol overhead, or other limitations.

Interview:

Throughput is the actual rate at which data is successfully transferred over a network.

4. Jitter

Jitter is the variation in packet arrival time.

Ideally, packets should arrive at relatively consistent intervals. If their arrival times vary significantly, jitter is high.

Normal:
Packet 1 → 10 ms
Packet 2 → 11 ms
Packet 3 → 10 ms

High Jitter:
Packet 1 → 10 ms
Packet 2 → 50 ms
Packet 3 → 15 ms
Why it matters

High jitter can cause problems in real-time applications such as:

Voice calls
Video conferencing
Online gaming
Live streaming

Interview:

Jitter is the variation in the delay between packets arriving at the destination.

5. Packet Loss ⭐

Packet loss occurs when packets sent across a network do not successfully reach their destination.

Packet 1 → Server ✅
Packet 2 → Server ❌
Packet 3 → Server ✅
Common causes
Network congestion
Faulty cables
Wireless interference
Hardware problems
Overloaded network devices
Configuration problems
Symptoms

Packet loss can cause:

Slow applications
Connection drops
Retransmissions
Poor voice/video quality
Timeouts
Basic troubleshooting
ping <destination>

You can use ping to observe whether packets are being lost between the source and destination.

Interview:

Packet loss occurs when packets fail to reach their intended destination.

6. MTU ⭐

MTU (Maximum Transmission Unit) is the largest packet size that can normally be transmitted over a network interface/link without fragmentation at that layer.

For standard Ethernet, a commonly encountered MTU is:

1500 bytes
Why MTU matters

If a packet is larger than the supported MTU, depending on the protocol and network configuration, it may need to be:

Fragmented
Dropped
Handled through Path MTU Discovery

Incorrect MTU settings can cause connectivity problems, especially when networks involve:

VPNs
Tunnels
Encapsulation
Cloud networking
AWS/DevOps relevance

MTU issues can sometimes appear in:

VPN connections
VPC networking
Containers
Overlay networks
Network tunnels

Interview:

MTU is the maximum size of a packet that can be transmitted over a network interface without requiring fragmentation at that point.

